me State and Strategy:**\n\nLet\'s break this down. I\'m in the Small Blind (SB), which means I act first pre-flop. The Big Blind is already in, so the pot is at 150. Since I\'m SB, I can\'t "check" (as indicated by the "0" action). I can Call 50 more (to match the Big Blind), raise (200+), or fold.\n\nGiven that the hand number is 10/20, I assume a standard Heads Up setup, with me as the Button (acting last post-flop) pre-flop. This is an important distinction!\n\nMy hand, K3 offsuit, is weak – a King high with a terrible kicker. However, in heads-up, even a weak hand can be playable, particularly when I have position (acting last post-flop) if the flop comes favorable. The 3:1 pot odds (150 in the pot, only 50 to call) look enticing.\n\n*   **Folding:** This would be too passive in heads-up play, giving up the pot too easily. With 50% equity against a random hand, it\'s not worth it to fold.\n*   **Raising:** I could raise to put pressure on my opponent and potentially win the pot pre-flop. However, with this weak hand, if my opponent re-raises, I\'m likely forced to fold post-flop which is not ideal.\n*   **Calling (Limping):** This seems like the most balanced and prudent play. It keeps the pot small, which is good with a weak hand, and allows me to see the flop in position. This lets me use my positional advantage without bloating the pot with an unfavorable hand.\n\n**The Decision:**\n\nConsidering everything, I\'m going to **call (0)**. I have a king high, and I\'ll keep the pot small to see what happens on the flop. The math works out in my favor due to the pot odds.\n\nThis should be the best play to extract some value on a semi-decent hand without going all in pre-flop.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation:\nThe hand K3o (King of Clubs, 3 of Diamonds) is a below-average hand, even in a Heads-Up (2-player) scenario. However, holding a King gives you decent high-card value. \n1. **Fold (-1):** Folding here would be too tight. You are in the Small Blind (Button) and only need to pay 50 chips to win a pot of 150. You have position post-flop, and K3o has enough equity against a random hand to justify seeing a flop cheaply.\n2. **Raise (200):** Raising attempts to steal the pot immediately. While aggressive, if the opponent calls or raises back, you are in a difficult spot with a weak kicker and no suit connection.\n3. **Call/Check (0):** Calling (limping) is the most prudent play. It keeps the pot small, allowing you to use your positional advantage post-flop. It realizes your equity for the cheapest price.\n\nReason:\nIn Heads-Up play, the Small Blind acts as the Button, giving you position throughout the hand. K3o is strong enough to play but weak enough that you don't want to build a large pot pre-flop. Limping (Action 0) allows you to see the flop cheaply and evaluate.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
