 by 786.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 1572 (raise the bet), or any value above 1572 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Analyzing a River Decision**\n\nOkay, so this is hand 10 out of 20, and I\'m down just a little bit from where I started, so not a huge deal yet. The pre-flop action is a little unclear, but I\'m pretty sure I was the small blind and checked, or maybe I just completed the big blind. The flop and turn were checked through, which is usually a sign of weakness from at least one player, and with my King-high, I\'m certainly in no great shape. Now the river is a Queen, which is scary, and my opponent bets 786 into a pot that was probably pretty small after all that checking.\n\nI\'ve got a King-high, which is essentially air at this point. There\'s no flush, no straight, nothing. My opponent checked the flop and turn, so they are either weak or trapping. The Queen on the river could have hit them hard, and a bet here suggests a pretty strong hand or a bluff. I have to think about pot odds. The bet is quite large relative to what\'s likely in the pot, but the historical checks make things murky.\n\nI really don\'t like my hand. K-high is just begging to be beat. The only hands I beat are complete air and even some air can win now. Given the pre-river action, they are likely value betting a small pair (8, 9, 7, 4) or the Queen, or they are bluffing. There\'s nothing I can do here. With my weak holding, I can\'t call, I can\'t raise, and I\'m not in a position to win a massive pot. While I\'ve been on a winning streak, it doesn\'t make sense to play for pride here. Even if I\'m "good" 10% of the time, that\'s not enough to justify calling this bet. I\'m going to fold and preserve my chips.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The current situation involves a River decision where I hold King-high (Kc 3d) on a board of 4d 9s 8d 7c Qh. The action has been checked down on the Flop and Turn. On the River, the opponent bets 786 chips. My hand beats missed straight draws (like 5-6 or J-T) or total air, but loses to any pair (including the river Queen, or previous pairs of 9, 8, 7, 4) or Ace-high. Given the check-down line, the river Queen is a card that often connects with an opponent's holdings (Qx hands). Calling a bet with just King-high is a low-percentage play without a strong read that the opponent is over-bluffing. Since I am currently in the lead cumulatively, preserving chips by folding a weak hand is a prudent strategy.\n\nReason: I hold only King High with no pair. The opponent has shown aggression on the River after a passive line, suggesting they may have hit the Queen or are betting a lower pair for value. The probability of winning at showdown is low. Folding saves chips for better opportunities.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
